"pmid">11169313</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yeh</surname><given-names>IT</given-names></name><name><surname>Dimitrov</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Otto</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Miller</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><name><surname>Kahlenberg</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Cruz</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pathologic review of atypical hyperplasia identified by image-guided breast needle core biopsy. Correlation with excision specimen</article-title><source>Arch Pathol Lab Med</source><year>2003</year><volume>127</volume><fpage>49</fpage><lpage>54</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Arch Pathol Lab Med||127|49||bib21|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12521366</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Multiplanar imaging based on a 3D-US volume data set during ultrasound-guided 14-gauge automated core biopsy proves that needle has transversed mass as central hit.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="jpeg" xlink:href="92-6602303f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Follow-up outcome of all breast lesions sampled by LCNB (<italic>n</italic>=962). <sup>&#x0002a;</sup>One patient refused excision biopsy despite LCNB-based DCIS diagnosis. <sup>&#x0002a;&#x0002a;</sup>One patient refused excision biopsy despite inconclusive LNCB diagnosis. <sup>&#x0002a;&#x0002a;&#x0002a;</sup>Three of these patients underwent subsequent surgery which confirmed benign lesions, all others were without any changes.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="92-6602303f2"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-